{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-second matchmaking connection wait, but this is a service or matchmaking delay rather than an in-game timer or wait-to-play mechanic, so T_PlayingByAppointment is rejected under the boundary rule. It mentions rewards being 'just for show' and difficulty obtaining weapon upgrades, but does not describe a specific reward-delivery or payment mechanic, so no reward or monetary label applies. Battling to win against others describes competitive gameplay, supporting S_Competition. The statement about being robbed of time is an evaluation and does not independently trigger Grinding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "stick battling to win",
      "rationale": "Describes competing in battles to win against other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review explicitly describes real-time battles against other players, which satisfies Competition. Strategy, building, expanding, graphics, and storyline do not describe any coded dark-pattern mechanic. No other candidate label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rationale": "This explicitly describes the game making players compete against other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked by hard levels and states that buying boosters is necessary to complete otherwise impossible tasks. This supports M_PayToProgress. T_Grinding is rejected because no repetitive activity is specified; T_InfiniteTreadmill is rejected because impossible tasks do not establish that the game itself has no attainable end state; no other label is triggered.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Plus there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters.",
      "rationale": "The span describes payment for boosters as necessary to complete tasks and progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a timed event whose stated remaining window was changed while the player was away, causing the player to lose access to a nearly completed reward. This satisfies Playing by Appointment because the event schedule and expiry determine when the player must play. FOMO was considered but rejected because the review does not describe anticipatory anxiety or continuing play to avoid missing out. Pay to Progress, Grinding, Daily Rewards, and Reward Mania were rejected because no payment gate, repetitive activity, daily-return reward, or chance-based delivery mechanic is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed",
      "rationale": "The game imposes a timed event schedule that changes while the player is away, affecting access to the pending reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a reward for inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, which triggers Forced Fellowship under the Social Pyramid Scheme indicator. It also describes chance-based gameplay and repeated duplicate sticker outcomes through \"played max bet\" and \"Stickers are duplicates all the way...\", supporting Reward Mania; Gambling is rejected because the review does not explicitly state that real money or purchased currency was wagered. The internet connection error is a technical fault, so it does not trigger a dark-pattern label under R11. No collection compulsion, competition, daily reward, or currency-conversion mechanic is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rationale": "Describes inviting friends to join the game/social platform in connection with an in-game reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rationale": "Describes repeated unwanted duplicate outcomes from a variable sticker reward system."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an interface that automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet table, directing the player toward a costly option and causing accidental mis-clicks. It also describes losing coins and being forced to buy real ones, which establishes a paid route to continue playing and real-money acquisition of in-game currency. I rejected M_EasyToPurchase because the review describes accidental loss of coins rather than an unintended real-money purchase or an insufficient-funds purchase prompt. I rejected S_Competition because the 1v1 table is mentioned only as the location of the interface behavior, not as a competition mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically directs attention and selection toward the highest-cost betting option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rationale": "The review describes payment as necessary to restore the currency needed to continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "forced to buy real ones",
      "rationale": "In context, 'ones' refers to coins, explicitly describing real-money acquisition of in-game currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an alleged manipulated outcome mechanic: the RNG is said to be against the player. This supports P_IllusionOfControl under the rule covering claims that the game manipulates or predetermines outcomes. I considered M_PayToProgress, but the review does not explicitly state that payment is required to advance; it only says playing without spending is unrewarding. M_IntermediateCurrency is rejected because no named currency is described as being bought with real money. Reward Mania is rejected because no specific random reward-delivery system, such as packs, rolls, or gacha, is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rationale": "This asserts that the game's random outcome determination is biased against the player."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an event where spending money is used to win, which satisfies Pay to Progress through the pay-to-win indicator, and it explicitly refers to winning an event, establishing player competition. The word \"grinder\" is only a general characterization without a named repetitive activity or explicit progress link, so T_Grinding is rejected under its boundary rule. No chance-based mechanic, currency conversion, ad viewing, or other coded mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes paying money to gain an advantage and win in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rationale": "Describes competition among players to win an event."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes paid advantages, base upgrade timers, endlessly scaling progression, deliberately confusing purchase-oriented UI, and player attacks associated with alliances. I considered T_Grinding but rejected it under the boundary rule because \"more grind\" does not identify a repeated activity. I considered S_ForcedFellowship but rejected it because being required to join an alliance for protection is coerced grouping without recruitment rewards or an obligation to avoid letting group members down. The alliance attacks support S_Competition, while the UI statement supports M_UIMisdirection because it explicitly attributes confusing design to directing players toward purchases.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes a design where spending provides an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rationale": "Describes base upgrade or build timers that impose waiting before progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly",
      "rationale": "States that the game's progression continually expands without an attainable stopping point."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rationale": "Attributes confusing interface design to directing players toward purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rationale": "Describes players being placed in adversarial conflict with other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes paying real money for extra in-game coins, which triggers Intermediate Currency. The advertised pin-pull content and claims of false advertising are outside the game-application scope under R13, so they do not trigger Advertisement or Reward Mania. Being over level 4000 does not establish an Infinite Treadmill. Connection problems after updates are technical faults, and no qualifying in-game wait or progress penalty is described, so no temporal label applies. Pay to Progress is rejected because the payment is not explicitly tied to advancement.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "I have also paid a lot for extra coins and things",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes paying real money to acquire in-game coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes accumulated in-game holdings being lost after leaving: the bag is full when the player exits, but empty on return, and earned and purchased coins have been consumed. This supports Easy to Get, Hard to Lose because leaving results in forfeiture of value already held. Waste Aversion was considered but rejected because no specific currency shortfall, misaligned price, or inventory-capacity tradeoff is described. Playing by Appointment was rejected because no game-imposed schedule or required return window is stated.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I leave the game with my bag full only to come back with it empty and coins that I earned and purchased used",
      "rationale": "Leaving the game causes accumulated inventory and currency to be lost, making it costly to stop playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchase prompts that appear before gameplay, payment being required for smooth progression, paid access to the newest bot instead of earning it, pressure to keep up with a shifting meta, and rewards tied to ads. I considered Power Creep but rejected it under its boundary rule because the review does not explicitly state that previously acquired items lost value. I also rejected Intermediate Currency because no real-money-to-virtual-currency conversion is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers directly intercept gameplay before the player can play, matching prompted buying and active purchase solicitation."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck!",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly contrasts progression with paying money, indicating that payment is required or strongly needed to advance."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes pressure to keep pace with the changing competitive game state or fall behind."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rationale": "The span describes free rewards being obtained through advertisements, indicating incentivized ad viewing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes inventory capacity being consumed by retained game parts and items, leaving too few usable slots, with spending required to buy parts to continue playing. This supports M_WasteAversion because the capped storage creates a concrete space shortfall and forces a choice involving held items. It also supports M_PayToProgress because the reviewer says playing requires spending money to obtain parts. No other label is supported: there is no repeated task, timed schedule, purchase prompt, chance mechanic, or social mechanic described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "98 spaces total, 72 are taken up by parts of generators or lower generators that I can't merge with higher generators yet or high level items like a corn that I am not going to delete",
      "rationale": "Capped storage is occupied by items the player will not delete, creating a concrete shortage of usable inventory space."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Not enough slots to store and play without spending $$ to buy parts",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes spending money on parts as necessary to continue playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repetitive rounds undertaken to collect photos and complete a set, supporting Grinding and Complete the Collection. It also describes spending coins after losing, but does not clearly establish real-money conversion into coins, a chance-based wager, a purchase prompt that reduces real-money spending friction, or a deliberate currency shortfall. The failure to receive the photo is presented as a broken or misleading outcome rather than an explicitly described game mechanic, so Gambling, Pay to Progress, Intermediate Currency, Easy to Purchase, Reward Mania, and Waste Aversion are rejected under R2, R4, and R11.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I play round after round to collect 'photos'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes repeating rounds to pursue in-game progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "to complete the set",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes pursuing completion of an in-game collection."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review explicitly describes repetitive resource gathering for leveling, so T_Grinding applies. It also describes alliances competing through attacks, so S_Competition applies. The larger alliances attacking smaller ones and taking their resources is asymmetric harmful behavior that benefits the aggressors, so S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior applies. The advertisement is external marketing and is out of scope under R13. Mentioning dedicated time and alliances does not by itself show reluctance to leave or forfeiture of accumulated value, so P_EasyToGetHardToLose is rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "It's just grinding resources trying not to get raided so you can level up",
      "rationale": "The player describes repetitive resource gathering as the activity required to level up."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "The bigger alliances all just attack the smaller alliances",
      "rationale": "The review describes alliances competing against one another through attacks."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior",
      "span": "The bigger alliances all just attack the smaller alliances and take their resources",
      "rationale": "The span describes stronger groups harming weaker groups while gaining their resources."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a payment barrier to continued play and explicitly names grinding. The missing mission credit is attributed to a functional failure, so it is suppressed under R11. The lack of instructions is a general usability complaint, and paying to unequip is not explicitly tied to progression, so neither receives a label. Shards and gems being hard to obtain does not establish real-money conversion or a concrete shortfall.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you almost have to buy stuff just to be able to keep playing",
      "rationale": "States that purchasing is nearly required to continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "the amount and of grinding you do in comparison to what you get",
      "rationale": "Explicitly describes grinding as the repetitive activity undertaken for game rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes timed bonuses continuing to expire while the player is away, which imposes a game-controlled schedule and fits Playing by Appointment. It also states that progressing or continuing to play sometimes requires buying, fitting Pay to Progress. The missing lives after purchasing a package is described as a delivery failure rather than an intentional mechanic, so R11 suppresses dark-pattern labels. The gold charge does not explicitly demonstrate stranded currency or a real-money-to-currency conversion, so Waste Aversion and Intermediate Currency are rejected. The package timing is mentioned as a complaint/request but does not clearly establish a paid pass or subscription mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The timed bonuses tick away even though I am not playing. I go to bed with bonuses to wake up with them almost gone or gone.",
      "rationale": "Bonuses expire according to the game's timer while the player is absent, forcing engagement on the game's schedule."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You cannot win enough gold to play without buying sometimes.",
      "rationale": "The review states that purchasing is sometimes necessary to obtain enough gold to continue playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes purchasing a deal to obtain new cards, receiving duplicates instead, and needing two cards to complete an album for a grand prize. The purchase is tied to advancing toward the album reward, so M_PayToProgress applies. The album-completion goal describes a collection-compulsion mechanic, so P_CompleteTheCollection applies. M_Gambling and P_RewardMania were considered but rejected because the review does not explicitly describe a chance-based stake or a random delivery system; it describes a deal that promised new cards but delivered duplicates. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "i bought a deal which promised i would get NEW cards",
      "rationale": "The reviewer paid for a deal intended to advance completion of the album."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "I had two cards left which would grant me the grand prize for completing an album",
      "rationale": "The span describes acquiring the remaining cards to complete an in-game collection for a reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes newer characters becoming necessary to deal damage as the meta changes, which indicates previously acquired characters lose practical value over time and triggers Power Creep. It also explicitly describes being forced to obtain new characters to perform effectively and calls the game pay to win, supporting Pay to Progress. Reduced rewards and lower quality are evaluations without a qualifying mechanic, and no separate Gambling or Reward Mania label is assigned because the review does not describe chance-based pulls or a monetary stake.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "basically forcing us to pull for new characters if we want to actually do damage",
      "rationale": "The span describes obtaining new characters as necessary for effective gameplay progression and performance."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "the Meta changes more and more every single day basically forcing us to pull for new characters if we want to actually do damage",
      "rationale": "The changing meta makes older characters insufficient for dealing damage, showing devaluation through newer releases."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game design where players are encouraged to attack one another, which satisfies Competition. It also explicitly describes a large advantage gap between pay-to-win and free-to-play players, satisfying Pay to Progress because spending grants an in-game advantage. The statements that the game is unlike its advertisements are out-of-game marketing and are excluded under R13. 'Micro transaction menus' does not describe a purchase-friction mechanism, and playing for a couple months does not show difficulty leaving, so Easy to Purchase and Easy to Get Hard to Lose are rejected.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there's a huge gap between p2w and f2p",
      "rationale": "The span describes a pay-to-win advantage over free-to-play players, indicating that spending money grants an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "players are encouraged to attack each other",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes players being directed into competition against one another."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
